{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"04","chapter_title":"Data Storytelling With Excel","title":"Pivot Tables as Summaries","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting pivot tables as summaries to Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-01","statement":"Choose the pivot aggregation (Sum, Count, or Average) that matches the stated question, and justify it in the artifact source note"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish a per-category pivot row from the Grand Total, and confirm the pivot is refreshed against a source range that covers all the data before quoting any figure"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-04","statement":"Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel","cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/03/03"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_calibration","cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_grand_total","cat100-supplement_03_04_lo_03_direct_check"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_grand_total","cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-03":["cat100-supplement_03_04_lo_03_direct_check"],"cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-04":["cat100-supplement_03_04_lo_03_direct_check"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Pivot Tables as Summaries\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Pivot Tables As Summaries, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/03-04-pivot-tables-as-summaries.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Pivot Tables as Summaries: Pivot Tables As Summaries, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n> **Optional reference** (after reading): Microsoft’s official guide, [Create a PivotTable to analyze worksheet data](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/create-a-pivottable-to-analyze-worksheet-data-a9a84538-bfe9-40a9-a8e9-f99134456576). Then return to the artifact and make the chart claim, unit label, and source note more precise.\n\nA pivot table turns hundreds of raw rows into a short summary without a single formula. Hand it a list of every order and it can answer \"how much did each region sell?\" or \"how many orders did we get per month?\" in a few drags. It is the fastest path from a messy log to a finding, which is why it is the workhorse of everyday spreadsheet analysis.\n\nThe power comes with a responsibility: a pivot summarizes by a rule you choose (sum, count, average), and choosing the wrong rule answers a different question than you asked. This section teaches the anatomy of a pivot, Rows, Values, and the aggregation setting, and the two traps that quietly produce wrong summaries: reading the Grand Total when you wanted a category, and trusting numbers from a source range that no longer covers all the data.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Choose the pivot aggregation (Sum, Count, or Average) that matches the stated question, and justify it in the artifact source note.\n- Distinguish a per-category pivot row from the Grand Total, and confirm the pivot is refreshed against a source range that covers all the data before quoting any figure.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nYou have a log of six orders across three regions and your boss asks two questions: which region brought in the most money, and which region placed the most orders. With raw rows you would be hand-adding and tallying. With a pivot you drag Region to Rows and Sales to Values, and West, East, South appear with their totals in seconds.\n\nThen the trap springs. You add a new order at the bottom of the log, glance at the pivot, and the totals have not budged; the pivot is reading a frozen snapshot until you refresh it. Or you report the Grand Total of 1900 when the question was about a single region. The pivot was fast, but fast and correct are not the same thing, and the gap between them is exactly what this section trains you to close.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA pivot table has a small set of zones, and once you know what each does, you can build almost any summary:\n\n| Zone | What you drag here | Effect on the summary |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Rows | A category field (Region, Month) | One row per distinct value |\n| Columns | A second category field | Splits each row into sub-columns (a cross-tab) |\n| Values | A number field (Sales) | The cells being aggregated |\n| Filters | Any field | Limits which source rows are included |\n\nThe decisive setting lives inside Values: the aggregation function. Drag Sales to Values and Excel defaults to Sum, which answers \"how much money?\" Open Value Field Settings and switch to Count and the same layout answers \"how many orders?\" Switch to Average and it answers \"what was the typical order size?\" Same fields, different aggregation, different question. Choosing the aggregation is therefore choosing the question your summary answers. (In Excel for the web, you reach the same setting via the arrow to the right of the value field name, or by right-clicking a value and choosing Summarize Values By, since the web version routes through the field dropdown rather than a separate dialog.)\n\nTwo structural facts govern correctness. A pivot reports per-category rows plus a Grand Total; the Grand Total is the all-data figure, so quoting it when you meant one category is a category error. And a pivot reads from a fixed source range captured when it was built, so new rows and edits do not appear until you click Refresh, and only if the source range still covers the data.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nStart with this raw order list of six rows.\n\n```\n   A       B       C\n1  Region  Order   Sales\n2  West    1001    400\n3  East    1002    250\n4  West    1003    600\n5  South   1004    150\n6  East    1005    300\n7  West    1006    200\n```\n\nStep 1. Select A1:C7 and insert a pivot table. Drag `Region` to Rows and `Sales` to Values. The default aggregation is Sum, so the pivot reports:\n\n```\nRegion   Sum of Sales\nEast     550\nSouth    150\nWest     1200\nGrand Total  1900\n```\n\nWest totals `400 + 600 + 200 = 1200`, and the Grand Total `1900` is the sum of all six orders, not a single region.\n\nStep 2. The question changes to \"how many orders per region?\" Open Value Field Settings and switch Sum to Count. The same layout now reports West = 3, East = 2, South = 1. Same fields, different question, different aggregation.\n\nStep 3. Add a seventh source row (South, 1007, 500) below the data. The totals do not move until you click Refresh, and they only move if the source range includes row 8. That is the stale-source-range trap.\n\nThe auditable decision: choose the aggregation that matches the question (Sum for dollars, Count for orders), read region rows rather than the Grand Total, and refresh a range that actually covers the data.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Build the region pivot and avoid the two traps\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You select A1:C7 and insert a pivot. To answer how much money each region brought in, what goes in Rows and Values, and which aggregation?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Sales in Rows, Region in Values, Average\",\"Region in Rows, Sales in Values, Sum\",\"Order in Rows, Sales in Values, Count\",\"Region in Rows, Order in Values, Sum\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Region to Rows, Sales to Values, aggregation Sum. The pivot reports West 1200, East 550, South 150, Grand Total 1900.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Your boss now asks how many ORDERS each region placed. What single change answers the new question?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Drag a new field into Filters\",\"Open Value Field Settings and switch Sum to Count\",\"Sort the rows alphabetically\",\"Read the Grand Total instead\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Switch the Values aggregation from Sum to Count. Same fields, different question: West 3, East 2, South 1.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Someone reports the top region sold 1900. Why is that wrong?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"1900 is the Grand Total of all regions, not one category; West is 1200\",\"1900 is correct, West is the biggest\",\"The pivot needs refreshing first\",\"1900 is the order count\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"1900 is the all-data Grand Total. Quoting it as one region is a category error. West’s actual figure is the named row, 1200.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Add a 7th row (South, 1007, 500). The totals do NOT move until you click Refresh, and only if the source range reaches the new row. That is the stale-source-range trap.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nMatch each business question to the aggregation that answers it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"How much money did each region bring in?\",\"definition\":\"Sum\"},{\"term\":\"How many orders did each region place?\",\"definition\":\"Count\"},{\"term\":\"What was the typical order size per region?\",\"definition\":\"Average\"},{\"term\":\"A row was added but the total did not change\",\"definition\":\"Click Refresh (and check the source range)\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nMatch the aggregation to the question, read the category row not the Grand Total, and refresh before you quote any number. A pivot is only as current as its last refresh and only as correct as its chosen aggregation.\n\nBefore you report a pivot figure, confirm three things:\n\n1. **Aggregation check:** Is the Values field set to the function the question needs (Sum for totals, Count for tallies, Average for typical size)?\n2. **Right-cell check:** Are you quoting the specific category row you were asked about, not the Grand Total that combines everything?\n3. **Freshness check:** Have you clicked Refresh since the last data edit, and does the source range still include the newest rows?\n\nIf a colleague added rows and your headline number did not change, you skipped the freshness check.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most frequent error is reporting the Grand Total as if it were a single category. If the pivot shows West = 1200, East = 550, South = 150, Grand Total = 1900, then \"the top region sold 1900\" is wrong; 1900 is the sum of all regions and West's actual figure is 1200. Always read down to the named row.\n\nA second misreading is assuming a pivot is live like a formula. It is not; it caches its source. Add a row and the pivot ignores it until you Refresh, and if the new row falls outside the original source range, even a refresh will miss it. Extending the source to cover all data, or basing the pivot on a Table, prevents this stale-range trap.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply pivots to a different log: a semester of study-group attendance, one row per session, with columns Course, Week, and Attendees. The question is \"which course drew the largest average group?\"\n\nDrag Course to Rows and Attendees to Values. The default aggregation is Sum, which would tell you total attendance, not the size of a typical session, so open Value Field Settings and switch to Average. Now each course row shows its mean group size, which is the question asked. If you had left it on Sum, a course that met twice as often would look more popular per session than it really was.\n\nRead the course rows, never the Grand Total, since an average of averages across courses is not a meaningful figure. Then add last week's sessions to the log and click Refresh; confirm the source range reached the new rows. The reported figure is now both the right statistic and the current one.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader interrogates a pivot before believing it, because a pivot can be confidently wrong. Ask:\n\n1. What aggregation is in the Values box, and does it answer the stated question?\n2. Is the quoted number a category row or the Grand Total?\n3. When was this pivot last refreshed, and does its source range cover every data row?\n\nWhen you can look at a summary and immediately ask \"sum or count?\" and \"refreshed when?\", you treat a pivot as evidence to be checked rather than an answer to be trusted.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A colleague added three orders to the bottom of the log this morning, but the pivot’s headline number is unchanged. What happened?\" explanation=\"A pivot caches its source range and is not live like a formula. New rows are ignored until you click Refresh, and if they fall outside the original source range, even a refresh misses them. This is the freshness check failing, not a data-entry error.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The new orders were entered incorrectly\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The pivot was not refreshed (and the source range may not reach the new rows)\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Pivots automatically ignore the last three rows\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries\", \"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong pivot artifact states which aggregation it uses and proves the headline against the source. The trace should name the question, the aggregation chosen, and a reconciliation step. A model line reads: \"The question was total sales per region, so Values is set to Sum; West shows 1200, which equals 400 plus 600 plus 200 from the three West rows in the source log, and I refreshed after adding the latest order.\"\n\nThe weak version pastes a pivot and quotes a number. The strong version lets a reviewer add the underlying rows by hand and reach the same figure, and shows that the source range and refresh state make the number current.\n\n**Recent tools (as of 2026):** Copilot in Excel will offer to build a PivotTable and pick an aggregation for you. Treat it as a suggestion, not an answer: run the three checks (the aggregation matches the question, you are reading a category row rather than the Grand Total, the refreshed range covers all the data) before you accept it. A defensible AI-use note records when you accepted the suggested pivot, when you switched its Sum to Count, and when you rejected its Grand-Total headline.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same pivot logic transfers to a two-dimensional cross-tab, where you use the Columns zone. Suppose the question becomes \"how do sales break down by region and by quarter at once?\" Drag Region to Rows, Quarter to Columns, and Sales to Values, and the pivot produces a grid: regions down the side, quarters across the top, each cell the sales for that region-quarter pair. The aggregation, Grand Total, and refresh rules all still apply, but now there are row totals and column totals, and confusing a row total (one region across all quarters) with a column total (one quarter across all regions) is the new version of the Grand-Total trap. Handling that grid correctly shows you understand the zones, not just the single-field summary.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/03-04-pivot-tables-as-summaries-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"03.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title for CAT 100 03.04. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries","cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel"],"stem":"You drag the 'Region' field to Rows and the 'Sales' field to Values in a pivot table. By default the Values area shows 'Sum of Sales'. What does each row now report?","options":["The number of orders in each region.","The total sales added up for each region.","The largest single sale in each region.","The alphabetical rank of each region."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Placing Sales in the Values area with the default Sum aggregation totals the Sales for every row that shares a Region. Each region row shows that subtotal.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_count_vs_sum","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries"],"stem":"Your pivot shows 'Sum of Sales' but you actually wanted the number of orders per region. What is the correct adjustment?","options":["Add the Sales field a second time to Rows.","Change the Value Field Setting for Sales from Sum to Count so each row reports how many orders occurred.","Sort the pivot table by region name.","Drag Region into the Values area instead."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Sum and Count answer different questions. Switching the value field setting to Count reports the number of records per region instead of their dollar total.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_count_vs_sum","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries","ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"You add 20 new rows to the source range below your pivot table, then click Refresh, but the new rows do not appear in the totals. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Pivot tables cannot be refreshed once created.","The pivot table's source range was fixed to the old rows and does not include the new data, so the range must be updated (or use a Table source).","Excel deleted the new rows during refresh.","The pivot table needs to be deleted and rebuilt every time."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A pivot table summarizes only the source range it was given. If new rows fall outside that fixed range, Refresh will not pull them in. Expand the range or base the pivot on an Excel Table so it grows automatically.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_stale_source_range","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"You want average sale size per region, not the total. Where do you change this in the pivot table?","options":["In the worksheet cell formulas outside the pivot.","In Value Field Settings, set 'Summarize Values By' to Average so each region shows its mean sale.","By sorting the region rows largest to smallest.","By adding Region to the Columns area as well as Rows."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The Value Field Settings dialog controls how a field is aggregated. Choosing Average makes each region row report the mean sale instead of the sum.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_count_vs_sum","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_grand_total","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries","stem":"Your pivot shows West 1,200, East 550, South 150, Grand Total 1,900. A teammate reports 'our top region sold 1,900.' What is wrong?","options":["Nothing; 1,900 is the West total.","1,900 is the all-data Grand Total across every region; the top region is West at 1,200. Quoting the Grand Total as one category is a category error.","The pivot needs refreshing before any number can be quoted.","1,900 is the order count, not the sales total."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The Grand Total row sums every category, so it can never be read as the value of one region. The top region is the largest category row (West, 1,200). Reading 1,900 as a region's sales is the Grand-Total-as-category error.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_grand_total_misread","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_pivot_tables_as_summaries"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100-supplement_03_04_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-03","cat100-supplement-03-04-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_count_vs_sum","pattern":"wrong_value_aggregation","description":"Reading whatever aggregation the pivot defaults to as the answer, without checking whether Sum, Count, or Average matches the question asked","trigger":"learner reports a pivot total without confirming the value field setting","feedback":"Match the aggregation to the question. Use Sum for totals, Count for how many records, and Average for typical size. Set this in Value Field Settings.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_stale_source_range","pattern":"stale_pivot_source_range","description":"Assuming a pivot table updates automatically when new rows are added, even though its source range was fixed to the original data","trigger":"learner adds rows and the pivot totals do not change after refresh","feedback":"A pivot only summarizes the range it was given. Expand the source range or base the pivot on an Excel Table so added rows are included on refresh.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_4_pivot_tables_as_summaries_grand_total_misread","pattern":"grand_total_as_per_category","description":"Reading the pivot Grand Total row as if it were a single category subtotal rather than the total across all categories","trigger":"learner cites the Grand Total when a per-region figure was needed","feedback":"The Grand Total combines every category. Read the individual region rows for per-category figures, not the bottom total.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"07b7e7b9b74922b50d864765d8a3c83efc9473c63e040e8b32eb5eb92fa306b2","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}